/Pg 23 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 265 0 R
/E (Full exemption: Effective April 1, 2024, the fair market value threshold for a full exemption for newly built homes is increased from $750,000 to $1,100,000.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 265 0 R
/E ( Partial exemption: A partial exemption is also available for properties with fair market values just above the threshold. The phase out range is $50,000 above the threshold, with the complete elimination of the exemption at $1,150,000 for qualifying purchasers. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 265 0 R
/T (DOWN PAYMENT )
/E (DOWN PAYMENT )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 265 0 R
/E (Your down payment is a crucial component of your home purchase. Depending on the lender and the property type, down payments can vary. As a general rule, for condos, townhouses, or detached houses, the minimum down payment is 5% of the first $500,000 and 10% of the portion between $500,000 and $1.5 million. Homes priced at $1.5 million or more require at least 20% down, and bare land purchases usually require at least 50%. If your down payment is less than 20%, you'll need mortgage default insurance \(through CMHC or a private insurer\). Premiums range from 2.80% to 4.00% of the loan amount depending on your down payment, plus 0.20% if you choose a 30-year amortization, which first-time buyers are eligible for. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 265 0 R
/T (OTHER COSTS )
/E (OTHER COSTS )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [19]
>>
